A ring $R$ is Noetherian if it satisfies any (hence all) of the following equivalent conditions:

1. every ideal of $R$ is finitely generated, $I = (a_1, \dots, a_r)$;
2. (ascending chain condition) every chain of ideals $I_1 \subseteq I_2 \subseteq I_3 \subseteq \cdots$ is eventually constant: there is $N$ with $I_n = I_N$ for all $n \ge N$;
3. (maximal condition) every nonempty collection of ideals of $R$ has a maximal element, an ideal in the collection not strictly contained in any other member.

In words: you cannot keep enlarging an ideal forever, and every ideal has a finite description.

